# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""NCERT Class 3 Mathematics - Chapter 1: What's in a Name? Slide Data"""

slides = [
    {'id': 1, 'title': 'Pre-Numerical Counting', 'badge': 'Historical Counting', 'sim_id': 'sim_prenum_cattle', 'description': 'Discover how ancient pastoral communities kept track of livestock herds through one-to-one correspondence before written numerals existed.', 'speech': 'Welcome young mathematicians to our exciting journey through the origins of counting and data. Long before ancient human civilizations invented written number symbols such as one, two, or three, early pastoral communities needed dependable practical methods to keep track of their valuable domestic animals. Imagine a humble cowherd family residing near a quiet countryside village. Every morning at sunrise, the village cowherds opened the sturdy wooden corral gates to lead their herd of cows out into the lush green pastures. Because these ancient cowherds had not yet learned formal counting words or modern arithmetic symbols, they relied upon a brilliant foundational mathematical principle known as one-to-one correspondence. Before departing into the open meadows, the cowherd placed a single smooth river pebble into a clay pot for each cow that stepped through the gate. If twelve cows walked past, exactly twelve pebbles rested inside the pot. In the evening, as each cow returned through the gate, the cowherd removed one pebble from the pot. If the pot was completely empty at dusk, the cowherd knew with absolute certainty that every single animal had returned safely. This ingenious physical matching strategy demonstrates how human beings intuitively organized mathematical quantities thousands of years ago.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'What fundamental mathematical principle allowed early pastoral cowherds to track cattle without numerals?', 'options': ['One-to-one correspondence between animals and physical markers', 'Memorizing multi-digit numerical formulas', 'Measuring the total weight of the cattle herd on a platform', 'Counting the footsteps made by cows in the mud'], 'ans': 'One-to-one correspondence between animals and physical markers', 'explanation': 'One-to-one correspondence pairs each individual object with exactly one token, allowing quantity verification without number words.'}, {'q': "If all pebbles were removed from the clay pot upon the herd's evening return, what did it signify?", 'options': ['All cattle had returned safely from the pasture', 'Several cattle were still lost in the forest', 'Extra stray animals had joined the herd', 'The clay pot was broken and unusable'], 'ans': 'All cattle had returned safely from the pasture', 'explanation': 'An empty pot proved that every cow that walked out in the morning had returned through the corral gate.'}]},
    {'id': 2, 'title': 'Wall Mark Tallying', 'badge': 'Tally Mark Origins', 'sim_id': 'sim_wall_tally', 'description': 'Explore how scratching vertical marks on a clay wall created durable visual records of outgoing and returning livestock.', 'speech': 'As pastoral communities continued tending livestock across changing seasons, carrying heavy bags of river pebbles became inconvenient. A creative village friend proposed a far more durable recording strategy using the smooth clay walls of the cattle shed. Early in the morning, as the young cowherds prepared to guide the cattle into the grazing fields, one cowherd stood vigilantly beside the corral boundary holding a piece of sharp white chalk or charcoal. Each time a cow walked through the wooden gateway toward the meadow, the cowherd scratched a distinct vertical tally mark on the wall. Ten cows passing through the doorway resulted in ten neat vertical strokes drawn side by side upon the earthen surface. When evening shadows lengthened and the grazing herd returned from the pasture, the recording process reversed systematically. For every cow that re-entered the shed, the cowherd drew a single diagonal strike through one vertical mark on the wall. By pairing each arriving animal with an existing tally line, the cowherds created a permanent visual record that could not spill or get lost. This simple yet brilliant wall-marking technique marks the earliest invention of tally charts in the history of human mathematics.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'How did the cowherds record each cow leaving the shed in the morning?', 'options': ['By drawing a single vertical mark on the clay wall', 'By carving a complex geometric picture of a cow', 'By writing the spoken name of each cow in ink', 'By tying a knot in a long silk thread'], 'ans': 'By drawing a single vertical mark on the clay wall', 'explanation': 'Each departing cow was quickly and reliably represented by a single vertical stroke made with chalk or charcoal.'}, {'q': 'What action was taken on the wall as each cow re-entered the shed in the evening?', 'options': ['Striking through one vertical mark to cancel it', 'Erasing the entire clay wall with water', 'Drawing two additional vertical marks beside it', 'Painting the entire wall with bright yellow dye'], 'ans': 'Striking through one vertical mark to cancel it', 'explanation': 'Drawing a diagonal line through a vertical stroke visually cancelled it, confirming that one animal had returned.'}]},
    {'id': 3, 'title': 'Tracking Missing Cattle', 'badge': 'Problem Solving', 'sim_id': 'sim_missing_cattle', 'description': 'Learn how uncrossed tally marks immediately alerted cowherds to missing animals, prompting targeted search and rescue.', 'speech': 'The true practical power of any mathematical recording system appears when unexpected real-world problems arise during daily work. Consider a dramatic evening when sunset cast long purple shadows across the Tarakeshwar village fields and farmland. The young cowherds had guided the cattle back from the distant riverside pasture and carefully cancelled marks on the shed wall. However, after all the cows inside the corral had been settled, the cowherds noticed something very important on the clay surface: two vertical marks remained completely uncrossed! Even without modern subtraction arithmetic, the physical evidence was unmistakable and urgent. Two uncancelled marks meant that exactly two cows had failed to return through the gate and remained somewhere out in the darkening pastures. Immediately realizing the danger, the village cowherds alerted the elders and organized a targeted search party carrying bright lanterns. They traced the grazing path back toward the riverbank, where they discovered two gentle cows grazing quietly near a thick grove of bamboo trees. The cowherds safely escorted the missing animals home, crossed out the final two wall marks, and rested peacefully. This real-life scenario proves how physical tally systems provide instant error detection and safeguard valuable resources without writing complex numerical equations.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'What did two uncrossed vertical marks on the wall indicate at the end of the day?', 'options': ['Exactly two cows were missing and still out in the pasture', 'Two extra cows had entered the village shed', 'The cowherds needed to feed the cattle twice as much grass', 'The chalk was completely worn out'], 'ans': 'Exactly two cows were missing and still out in the pasture', 'explanation': 'Because every returned cow cancelled one mark, any remaining uncrossed marks represented unreturned, missing animals.'}, {'q': 'Why is the tally cancellation method an effective mathematical monitoring tool?', 'options': ['It reveals discrepancies immediately without needing complex calculations', 'It changes the physical color of the animals in the dark', 'It prevents rain from falling on the cattle shed', 'It makes the cattle walk much faster towards the gate'], 'ans': 'It reveals discrepancies immediately without needing complex calculations', 'explanation': 'Visual tally matching instantly exposes differences between departing and arriving quantities at a glance.'}]},
    {'id': 4, 'title': 'Modern Tally Marks', 'badge': 'Grouping & Bundling', 'sim_id': 'sim_five_bundle_tally', 'description': 'Understand how grouping loose vertical marks into bundles of five revolutionized rapid counting and quantity recognition.', 'speech': 'While individual vertical marks worked wonderfully for small herds of eight or nine cows, managing larger quantities soon created a serious optical challenge for the observers. When thirty or forty identical vertical lines were drawn close together across an earthen wall, the human eye struggled to count them rapidly without losing place or making accidental recounting mistakes. To overcome this visual confusion, ancient mathematicians developed the brilliant convention of grouping tallies into standardized bundles of five units each. In this modern tally system, we draw four upright vertical lines side by side: one, two, three, and four. When the fifth item arrives, instead of drawing a fifth loose vertical line, we draw a prominent diagonal slash that crosses through all four previous strokes from top-left to bottom-right! This completed bundle instantly represents a closed group of five units. Counting large numbers now becomes completely effortless because our minds can swiftly skip-count by fives: five, ten, fifteen, twenty, twenty-five, thirty, and beyond! If we observe three completed bundles alongside two single vertical strokes, we immediately calculate five plus five plus five plus two, which totals seventeen items in a mere second. Bundling transforms messy counting into organized, efficient mathematical thinking.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'How is the number five represented in the standard modern tally mark system?', 'options': ['Four vertical lines crossed diagonally by a fifth slash', 'Five separate vertical lines drawn with wide gaps', 'A square box with a small circle inside it', 'Five horizontal lines stacked on top of each other'], 'ans': 'Four vertical lines crossed diagonally by a fifth slash', 'explanation': 'A complete tally bundle consists of four upright vertical strokes locked together by one diagonal fifth slash.'}, {'q': 'What is the primary advantage of bundling tally marks into groups of five?', 'options': ['It enables rapid skip-counting by fives without recounting individual marks', 'It uses less chalk on the surface of the blackboard', 'It makes the lines appear invisible in bright sunlight', 'It turns the marks into letters of the English alphabet'], 'ans': 'It enables rapid skip-counting by fives without recounting individual marks', 'explanation': 'Bundling allows our brains to skip-count by fives (5, 10, 15...) which is vastly faster and less error-prone than single counting.'}]},
    {'id': 5, 'title': 'Letter Count Teams', 'badge': 'Data Sorting', 'sim_id': 'sim_animal_letter_teams', 'description': 'Classify animals into balanced sports teams by calculating the exact letter lengths of their written common names.', 'speech': 'Let us now apply our growing mathematical counting skills to an entertaining forest tournament called the Team Selection Game! Imagine ten diverse animal friends gathering in an open forest clearing to play an exciting game of ball. The animals include an ox, a rat, a dog, a lion, a deer, a goat, a tiger, a snake, a monkey, and a grand elephant. To divide themselves fairly into two equal teams, the forest animals decide to use the letter counts of their written English names. First, the animals count the exact number of letters in each name: ox has two letters; rat and dog each possess three letters; lion, deer, and goat contain four letters each; tiger and snake have five letters; monkey contains six letters; and elephant boasts eight letters! The animals then establish a clear classification rule: creatures with short names containing four or fewer letters form Team A, while creatures with long names containing five or more letters join Team B. Team A proudly gathers ox, rat, dog, lion, deer, and goat, giving them six members, while Team B assembles tiger, snake, monkey, and elephant, totaling four members. Counting letters transforms everyday words into measurable numerical data for fair logical sorting.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': "How many letters are in the animal name 'ELEPHANT'?", 'options': ['8 letters', '7 letters', '6 letters', '9 letters'], 'ans': '8 letters', 'explanation': 'Counting the individual letters E-L-E-P-H-A-N-T reveals a total of exactly eight letters.'}, {'q': 'If animals with 4 or fewer letters join Team A, which animal correctly belongs to Team A?', 'options': ['GOAT (4 letters)', 'MONKEY (6 letters)', 'TIGER (5 letters)', 'SNAKE (5 letters)'], 'ans': 'GOAT (4 letters)', 'explanation': 'G-O-A-T has 4 letters, satisfying the condition of having four or fewer letters for Team A.'}]},
    {'id': 6, 'title': 'Name Length Analysis', 'badge': 'Comparative Measurement', 'sim_id': 'sim_name_length_ruler', 'description': 'Analyze classmate names using character length rulers to determine minimum, maximum, and average letter counts.', 'speech': "When young learners examine the names of their classmates and friends, they uncover delightful mathematical patterns hidden in plain sight. Every person's name is constructed from a specific sequence of alphabet letters, and counting these letters allows us to compare name lengths just like measuring physical objects with a sturdy wooden ruler. Suppose a classroom teacher asks a group of children to write down five common Indian names on neat display cards: Om, Joy, Aman, Priya, and Shrinathan. Looking closely at each name card, the observant students count the alphabet letters systematically one by one. The shortest name in the classroom sample is Om, consisting of just two letters: O and M. Next comes Joy with three letters, followed by Aman with four letters, and Priya with five letters. At the opposite end of the spectrum, the longest name in the group is Shrinathan, containing ten complete letters! By arranging these names in ascending order of letter count, children observe a clear mathematical progression: two, three, four, five, and ten. Comparing name lengths teaches children how qualitative text can be converted into quantitative numerical data, enabling them to calculate differences, identify extremes, and understand statistical range in primary school mathematics.", 'checkpoints': [{'q': "Between the names 'OM' (2 letters) and 'SHRINATHAN' (10 letters), what is the difference in letter count?", 'options': ['8 letters', '6 letters', '12 letters', '5 letters'], 'ans': '8 letters', 'explanation': 'Subtracting the shorter name length from the longer gives: 10 - 2 = 8 letters difference.'}, {'q': 'Which of the following names has exactly five letters?', 'options': ['PRIYA', 'JOY', 'AMAN', 'OM'], 'ans': 'PRIYA', 'explanation': 'P-R-I-Y-A contains exactly five alphabet letters.'}]},
    {'id': 7, 'title': 'Alphabet Letter Frequencies', 'badge': 'Letter Distribution', 'sim_id': 'sim_letter_frequency_sorter', 'description': 'Investigate starting letters, terminal endings, and repeating vowels across classroom name registries.', 'speech': 'Beyond simply measuring overall name lengths, young statisticians can explore the internal distribution of individual alphabet characters. In every classroom attendance register across India, certain letters appear with remarkable frequency at the start or end of names. For instance, when examining a list of twenty students, children often discover that several names begin with the same initial letter, such as the letter A in Aarav, Ananya, Amit, and Anvi. Similarly, the letter S frequently introduces popular names like Sanjay, Sunil, Simran, and Shreya. By categorizing names according to their starting letter, students can create an initial-letter frequency tally chart. Furthermore, examining terminal ending letters reveals intriguing linguistic and mathematical tendencies in our regional languages: many Indian feminine names conclude with the vowel letter A or I, as seen in Priya, Neha, Pooja, and Anjali, whereas many masculine names conclude with consonants like N, R, or L, such as in Rohan, Karan, and Rahul. Students can count the frequency of each vowel—A, E, I, O, and U—across their complete friend list. Discovering which letter appears most often provides young minds with their very first introduction to the fundamental statistical concept of mode in primary data analysis.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': "What initial letter is shared by the names 'AARAV', 'ANANYA', and 'AMIT'?", 'options': ['The letter A', 'The letter S', 'The letter M', 'The letter R'], 'ans': 'The letter A', 'explanation': 'All three names begin with the capital alphabet letter A.'}, {'q': 'In statistics, what term describes the letter or item that appears with the highest frequency in a data set?', 'options': ['The mode', 'The perimeter', 'The denominator', 'The quotient'], 'ans': 'The mode', 'explanation': 'The mode is the statistical value or category that occurs most frequently within an observed data set.'}]},
    {'id': 8, 'title': 'Number Name Cards', 'badge': 'Word Lengths', 'sim_id': 'sim_number_name_cards', 'description': 'Construct number word cards and discover the fascinating mathematical contrast between numerical value and letter count.', 'speech': "A captivating mathematical puzzle emerges when children compare the numerical value of a number with the letter count of its written name. Imagine two curious classroom students constructing colorful flashcards to represent quantities using English words and tally strokes. They write the word 'ten' on one card, 'seventeen' on another card, and 'forty-three' on a third card. At first glance, young children might intuitively expect that a bigger numerical value always requires a longer word. However, careful counting reveals surprising mathematical exceptions that spark lively classroom inquiry and logical discussions among learners! For example, consider the number ten: its numerical value is a two-digit quantity, yet its written name T-E-N has only three letters. Now consider the number seventeen: its value is seventeen, and its written name S-E-V-E-N-T-E-E-N contains nine distinct letters. Now examine seventy: its value is seventy, yet its word S-E-V-E-N-T-Y has only seven letters, which is fewer letters than seventeen! Even more remarkably, the number one hundred has an impressive value, but its written name contains ten letters, whereas ninety-eight has eleven letters. Analyzing number names proves that language word structure follows phonetic spelling rules distinct from numerical magnitudes in primary mathematics.", 'checkpoints': [{'q': "How many letters are in the written English word for the number 'SEVENTEEN'?", 'options': ['9 letters', '7 letters', '10 letters', '8 letters'], 'ans': '9 letters', 'explanation': 'Counting S-E-V-E-N-T-E-E-N gives exactly 9 alphabet letters.'}, {'q': 'Does a larger numerical value always have more letters in its written name than a smaller number?', 'options': ['No, word length depends on language spelling rather than numerical magnitude', 'Yes, every larger number always has more letters', 'Yes, every tens number has exactly 20 letters', 'No, all number words have exactly four letters'], 'ans': 'No, word length depends on language spelling rather than numerical magnitude', 'explanation': 'For example, 70 (seventy = 7 letters) has a greater numerical value than 17 (seventeen = 9 letters), but fewer letters.'}]},
    {'id': 9, 'title': 'Extreme Place Names', 'badge': 'Geographical Extremes', 'sim_id': 'sim_extreme_names_map', 'description': "Explore India's geographical extremes by investigating the railway station with the longest name and towns with the shortest names.", 'speech': "Our vast and diverse nation of India holds breathtaking geographical wonders across every state, including astonishing extremes in the letter lengths of its official railway station and town names. Young geographers and mathematicians will be thrilled to learn about an extraordinary railway station located in Andhra Pradesh, nestled near the southern border of Tamil Nadu: Venkatanarasimharajuvaripeta! When inquisitive students count every individual alphabet character in this magnificent railway board name, they discover that it contains an astounding twenty-eight letters, making it the longest railway station name in all of India. At the opposite end of our country's geographical spectrum lie two remarkable railway stations with the shortest names in India: the famous railway station of Ib, situated in the eastern state of Odisha beside the scenic Ib River, and the peaceful town of Od, located in the western state of Gujarat. Both Ib and Od contain just two alphabet letters each! Comparing these real-world extremes offers an exciting mathematical subtraction exercise for young minds: subtracting two letters from twenty-eight letters reveals an immense difference of twenty-six letters between India's longest and shortest place names. Investigating national geography through letter counting proves how mathematical data collection connects directly to our vibrant homeland.", 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'What is the longest railway station name in India, containing 28 letters?', 'options': ['Venkatanarasimharajuvaripeta', 'Thiruvananthapuram', 'Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Terminus', 'Visakhapatnam'], 'ans': 'Venkatanarasimharajuvaripeta', 'explanation': 'Venkatanarasimharajuvaripeta in Andhra Pradesh has 28 letters, making it the longest railway station name in India.'}, {'q': 'Which of the following represents one of the shortest place names in India with only 2 letters?', 'options': ['Ib (in Odisha)', 'Goa', 'Puri', 'Diu'], 'ans': 'Ib (in Odisha)', 'explanation': 'Ib in Odisha and Od in Gujarat have only two letters each, representing the shortest place names in India.'}]},
    {'id': 10, 'title': 'Number Word Riddles', 'badge': 'Logical Deduction', 'sim_id': 'sim_number_word_riddles', 'description': 'Solve clever mathematical riddles that combine linguistic letter counts with place value arithmetic clues.', 'speech': "Mathematical deduction becomes truly thrilling when we combine place value arithmetic clues with word and letter quantities. Let us examine an intriguing number puzzle frequently solved in primary classrooms: 'I am a two-digit number. My written English name consists of exactly two separate words. My first word has five letters, my second word has five letters, and my digits add up to seven. What number am I?' To solve this riddle, a young mathematician works like a brilliant scientific detective. First, the student analyzes the tens word: forty has five letters, and fifty has five letters. Next, the student examines the ones word: three has five letters, seven has five letters, and eight has five letters. Now let us test the digit sum condition where tens digit plus ones digit must equal seven. Consider fifty-two: two has three letters, so it cannot be fifty-two. Now consider forty-three: the tens digit is four, and the ones digit is three. Adding four plus three gives exactly seven! Furthermore, forty has five letters, and three has five letters, matching every single condition of the puzzle perfectly. Solving number riddles sharpens analytical problem-solving skills across mathematics and linguistics in joyful learning.", 'checkpoints': [{'q': "How many letters are in the tens word 'FORTY'?", 'options': ['5 letters', '6 letters', '4 letters', '7 letters'], 'ans': '5 letters', 'explanation': 'Counting the letters F-O-R-T-Y confirms exactly 5 letters.'}, {'q': "In the number 'FORTY-THREE', what is the sum of its two digits (4 and 3)?", 'options': ['7', '6', '8', '9'], 'ans': '7', 'explanation': 'Adding the tens digit 4 and ones digit 3 gives: 4 + 3 = 7.'}]},
    {'id': 11, 'title': 'Proximity To Hundred', 'badge': 'Number Line Intervals', 'sim_id': 'sim_hundred_proximity_line', 'description': 'Investigate number line intervals and symmetrical distances from the landmark century baseline of one hundred.', 'speech': "In primary arithmetic, the number one hundred serves as a magnificent psychological and mathematical milestone. Children love solving proximity riddles that challenge them to deduce secret numbers based on their distance from one hundred. Imagine a mathematical riddle that asks: 'I am an even number less than one hundred. If you take four steps forward from me on the number line, you will land precisely on one hundred. What number am I?' To solve this mystery, the student visualizes a horizontal number line ending at one hundred. Walking four steps forward means that our mystery number is exactly four units less than one hundred. Calculating one hundred minus four immediately reveals the answer: ninety-six! Now consider a companion riddle: 'I am greater than one hundred, and my distance from one hundred is identical to the distance between ninety-six and one hundred.' Since ninety-six is four units below one hundred, our new number must be four units above one hundred, which gives one hundred plus four, equaling one hundred and four! Notice how ninety-six and one hundred and four form a perfectly balanced, symmetrical pair around one hundred on the number line. Exploring distance from benchmark numbers develops strong mental subtraction and visual estimation abilities.", 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'Which number is located exactly 4 units before 100 on the number line?', 'options': ['96', '94', '98', '92'], 'ans': '96', 'explanation': 'Subtracting 4 from 100 gives: 100 - 4 = 96.'}, {'q': 'What number is located at the same distance above 100 as 96 is below 100?', 'options': ['104', '106', '102', '108'], 'ans': '104', 'explanation': 'Because 96 is 4 units below 100, the symmetrical number 4 units above 100 is: 100 + 4 = 104.'}]},
    {'id': 12, 'title': 'Living Things Classification', 'badge': 'Categorical Logic', 'sim_id': 'sim_living_eats_sorter', 'description': 'Classify diverse illustrated objects into mutually exclusive groups based on whether they require biological food to survive.', 'speech': 'Data handling in primary mathematics begins with the essential scientific skill of classification and categorical sorting. Before we can count, tally, or construct visual charts, we must first sort objects into clear, well-defined groups. Suppose a classroom teacher presents children with eight colorful picture cards showing diverse everyday items from nature and home: a cow, a wooden chair, a sparrow, a clay pot, an elephant, a school bag, a goldfish, and a plastic pencil box. The teacher asks the students to sort these picture cards into two distinct categories: living organisms that eat food and grow, versus non-living objects that do not eat food. The students examine each card with logical precision and scientific observation: the cow eats fresh grass, the sparrow pecks grain seeds, the elephant consumes sugarcane and leaves, and the goldfish eats water plants. All four of these living creatures belong together in Category A: Things That Eat Food! In contrast, the wooden chair, clay pot, school bag, and plastic pencil box are non-living human-made artifacts. They do not breathe, eat food, or grow, placing them squarely in Category B. Each group contains exactly four items, demonstrating how logical categorization creates balanced, orderly data sets ready for numerical analysis.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': "Which of the following items belongs in the category 'Things That Eat Food'?", 'options': ['SPARROW', 'WOODEN CHAIR', 'CLAY POT', 'SCHOOL BAG'], 'ans': 'SPARROW', 'explanation': 'A sparrow is a living bird that consumes grains and seeds for nutrition.'}, {'q': 'Why is sorting items into clear categories an important first step in mathematical data handling?', 'options': ['It organizes unstructured items into distinct groups that can be counted and compared', 'It changes the physical shape of the objects permanently', 'It makes all the objects completely weightless', 'It turns pictures into sound vibrations'], 'ans': 'It organizes unstructured items into distinct groups that can be counted and compared', 'explanation': 'Categorization creates well-defined subsets, which is the foundational basis for tallying and statistical analysis.'}]},
    {'id': 13, 'title': 'Electric Appliance Sorting', 'badge': 'Technology Sorting', 'sim_id': 'sim_electric_manual_sorter', 'description': 'Sort household devices into electrical appliances versus manual tools to practice multi-attribute data classification.', 'speech': 'Let us deepen our classification expertise by exploring modern technological objects found inside our homes and school classrooms. Consider another exciting sorting task where students examine a collection of eight common household instruments and appliances: a ceiling fan, a manual wooden broom, a refrigerator, a stainless steel hand spoon, a television set, a plastic water bucket, a table lamp, and an iron claw hammer. The mathematical challenge is to classify these items into two mutually exclusive sets: devices that require electrical energy from a wall socket or chemical battery to function, versus manual mechanical tools that operate solely through human physical effort and muscle power. The students test each device thoughtfully: the ceiling fan requires electricity to spin its blades; the refrigerator needs electrical power to run its cooling compressor; the television relies on electric currents to display pictures; and the table lamp needs electricity to light its bulb. These four items form the Electrical Appliances set. Meanwhile, the broom, hand spoon, water bucket, and claw hammer require zero electric current, relying entirely on human muscle power to sweep, scoop, carry, and strike. Grouping items by functional properties reinforces how binary classification organizes real-world technology into clean mathematical subsets.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': "Which of the following household items belongs to the 'Requires Electricity' group?", 'options': ['REFRIGERATOR', 'WOODEN BROOM', 'CLAW HAMMER', 'WATER BUCKET'], 'ans': 'REFRIGERATOR', 'explanation': 'A refrigerator requires electrical energy to power its motor and cool food.'}, {'q': 'How many items belong to the manual mechanical group if 4 out of 8 items require electricity?', 'options': ['4 items', '2 items', '6 items', '8 items'], 'ans': '4 items', 'explanation': 'Subtracting 4 electrical items from 8 total items leaves exactly 4 manual items.'}]},
    {'id': 14, 'title': 'Hairstyle Data Collection', 'badge': 'Observational Data', 'sim_id': 'sim_hairstyle_collector', 'description': 'Gather live classroom data by observing and tallying the diverse hairstyles worn by students in a class.', 'speech': "Now that our young mathematicians understand tallying, letter counting, and categorical sorting, they are fully prepared to conduct a genuine statistical survey right inside their own bustling classroom! In Chapter 1, students conduct a delightful observational study focused on their classmates' diverse and charming hairstyles. Looking around the lively classroom, the observant students notice that hair can be styled in several distinct, recognizable ways: some children wear two perky ponytails tied neatly with colorful ribbons; others wear a single sleek ponytail hanging down their back; some children have traditionally braided hair; many students sport short cropped haircuts; and some display naturally curly locks of hair. To record this real-world information systematically without making errors, the student surveyor creates an observational tally sheet. As the surveyor observes each classmate one by one, a tally stroke is immediately recorded in the corresponding hairstyle column. If five students wear two ponytails, the student draws four vertical lines crossed by one diagonal slash. Collecting live observational data teaches children that mathematics is not just abstract arithmetic written in notebooks, but a dynamic, joyful, and creative tool for observing, recording, and celebrating the wonderful diversity of the human world around them.", 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'What is the primary purpose of conducting a classroom hairstyle survey?', 'options': ['To collect, tally, and organize real-world observational data', 'To force all children to have the same haircut', 'To measure the height of the classroom walls', 'To test the speed of running in the playground'], 'ans': 'To collect, tally, and organize real-world observational data', 'explanation': 'A survey allows students to practice gathering empirical data from their immediate environment using tally marks.'}, {'q': 'If an observer spots 5 children with braided hair, how is that quantity recorded with tallies?', 'options': ['Four vertical lines crossed by one diagonal slash', 'Five separate circles drawn in a row', 'Five long horizontal strokes', 'A single large cross'], 'ans': 'Four vertical lines crossed by one diagonal slash', 'explanation': 'A quantity of five is always represented as a closed five-bundle: four upright lines with one diagonal strike.'}]},
    {'id': 15, 'title': 'Hairstyle Frequency Table', 'badge': 'Tabular Synthesis', 'sim_id': 'sim_hairstyle_tally_table', 'description': 'Convert raw observational tallies into a structured two-column frequency table to discover statistical insights.', 'speech': 'Once all observational tally marks have been carefully recorded across the classroom, the next vital step in statistical analysis is summarizing raw data into a structured frequency table. A frequency table is an organized grid containing two primary columns: the descriptive category name on the left side, and the total numerical count, called the frequency, on the right side. Suppose a classroom survey of twenty-five students yields the following tally counts: eight students have short hair, represented by one full five-bundle plus three vertical strokes; six students wear two ponytails, represented by one five-bundle plus one single stroke; five students wear neat braids, represented by one complete five-bundle; four students wear a single ponytail, represented by four vertical strokes; and two students have curly hair, represented by two neat strokes. By translating each set of tally bundles into clear numerical digits, students instantly observe that short hair is the most common hairstyle with a frequency of eight, while curly hair is the least common hairstyle with a frequency of two. Summing the frequencies confirms that eight plus six plus five plus four plus two equals twenty-five total students, proving that no child was accidentally missed or counted twice in the survey.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'In the survey, which hairstyle had the highest frequency with 8 students?', 'options': ['Short hair', 'Two ponytails', 'Braids', 'Curly hair'], 'ans': 'Short hair', 'explanation': 'Short hair had 8 students, making it the most frequent hairstyle in the classroom survey.'}, {'q': 'What is the total number of students surveyed if frequencies are 8, 6, 5, 4, and 2?', 'options': ['25 students', '20 students', '30 students', '22 students'], 'ans': '25 students', 'explanation': 'Adding all frequencies together: 8 + 6 + 5 + 4 + 2 = 25 total students.'}]},
    {'id': 16, 'title': 'Pictograph Data Displays', 'badge': 'Visual Charts', 'sim_id': 'sim_pictograph_chart_builder', 'description': 'Transform numerical frequency tables into vibrant visual pictographs where illustrated icons represent quantities.', 'speech': "To communicate statistical findings clearly and engagingly to others, mathematicians transform numbers into visual charts called pictographs! A pictograph is a delightful graphical display where small pictures or illustrated symbols represent numerical quantities in rows or columns. In our classroom hairstyle study, we can design a friendly smiling face icon to represent individual student counts. First, every successful pictograph requires a clear key or scale: let one smiling face symbol represent exactly one student. Beside the 'Short Hair' label, we draw a neat horizontal row of eight smiling face symbols. Beside 'Two Ponytails', we line up six smiling faces. Beside 'Braids', we place five smiling faces. Beside 'Single Ponytail', we draw four smiling faces. And beside 'Curly Hair', we display two smiling faces. Looking at the completed pictograph, even a young child who has not yet learned to read numbers can instantly understand the survey results! The longest horizontal row of faces immediately identifies the most popular hairstyle, while the shortest row highlights the least frequent style at a quick glance. Pictographs bridge the gap between abstract numbers and intuitive visual understanding, empowering young learners to present scientific and mathematical data with genuine confidence, clarity, and creative joy.", 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'What is a pictograph?', 'options': ['A chart that uses pictures or symbols to represent numerical data', 'A machine that takes photographs of distant stars', 'A musical instrument played in an orchestra', 'A special pen used for writing upside down'], 'ans': 'A chart that uses pictures or symbols to represent numerical data', 'explanation': 'A pictograph uses repeatable picture symbols or icons to visually represent numerical quantities.'}, {'q': 'If one smiling face icon represents 1 student, how many face icons are drawn for 6 students with two ponytails?', 'options': ['6 face icons', '3 face icons', '12 face icons', '1 face icon'], 'ans': '6 face icons', 'explanation': 'With a 1-to-1 scale key, 6 students require exactly 6 smiling face icons.'}]},
    {'id': 17, 'title': 'Challenge 1', 'badge': 'Mastery Challenge', 'description': 'Test your mastery of historical one-to-one correspondence, wall marks, missing livestock tracking, and 5-bundle tallies.', 'questions': [{'id': 'c1_q1', 'q': 'What method did ancient cowherds use to keep track of their cattle before numerals were invented?', 'options': ['One-to-one correspondence using pebbles in a pot', 'Writing complex equations on tree bark', 'Measuring the volume of milk produced daily', 'Taking photographs of each animal with a camera'], 'ans': 'One-to-one correspondence using pebbles in a pot', 'hint': 'Think about placing one physical object for each departing cow.', 'explanation': 'Before written numerals existed, pairing each cow with a single river pebble in a pot ensured complete tracking.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Identify the historical counting technique used by pastoral cowherds.', 'step2': 'Recognize that one pebble was added for every departing cow and removed upon return.', 'step3': 'Conclude that this represents one-to-one physical correspondence.'}}, {'id': 'c1_q2', 'q': 'If 14 cows leave the cattle shed in the morning, how many vertical scratch marks are drawn on the clay wall?', 'options': ['14 vertical marks', '10 vertical marks', '7 vertical marks', '28 vertical marks'], 'ans': '14 vertical marks', 'hint': 'Each cow that departs is represented by exactly one vertical stroke.', 'explanation': 'Because one vertical stroke corresponds to one cow, 14 departing cows produce 14 vertical marks.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Recall the wall tally rule: 1 cow = 1 vertical scratch mark.', 'step2': 'Multiply 14 cows by 1 mark per cow.', 'step3': 'Deduce that exactly 14 vertical marks must be drawn.'}}, {'id': 'c1_q3', 'q': 'In the evening, 11 cows return and are struck through on the wall. If 14 marks were drawn in the morning, how many marks remain uncrossed?', 'options': ['3 marks', '5 marks', '2 marks', '4 marks'], 'ans': '3 marks', 'hint': 'Subtract the number of returned cows from the original total.', 'explanation': '14 original marks minus 11 returned cows leaves 14 - 11 = 3 uncrossed marks.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Identify the initial number of marks drawn (14).', 'step2': 'Subtract the number of returned cows crossed out (11).', 'step3': '14 - 11 = 3 uncrossed marks remaining on the wall.'}}, {'id': 'c1_q4', 'q': 'What do uncrossed marks on the shed wall indicate to the cowherds at dusk?', 'options': ['Animals that are missing and have not yet returned from the pasture', 'Extra stray animals that joined the herd', 'That the cowherds should feed the animals double grass', 'That the clay wall needs to be washed with water'], 'ans': 'Animals that are missing and have not yet returned from the pasture', 'hint': 'Marks are crossed out only when cows enter the gate.', 'explanation': 'Since each returning cow cancels one mark, any uncrossed mark represents an animal still out in the fields.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Understand the two-way cancellation process.', 'step2': 'A cancelled mark means safe return; an uncancelled mark means no return.', 'step3': 'Conclude that uncrossed marks signal missing animals requiring search.'}}, {'id': 'c1_q5', 'q': 'How many vertical lines are drawn before the diagonal fifth slash crosses through them in a standard tally bundle?', 'options': ['4 vertical lines', '5 vertical lines', '3 vertical lines', '6 vertical lines'], 'ans': '4 vertical lines', 'hint': 'The fifth stroke is the diagonal line crossing previous upright strokes.', 'explanation': 'A complete tally bundle of 5 consists of 4 vertical lines locked by 1 diagonal slash.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Review the anatomy of a standard tally bundle.', 'step2': 'Count the upright strokes drawn first: 1, 2, 3, 4.', 'step3': 'The 5th stroke is the diagonal line, so 4 vertical lines precede it.'}}, {'id': 'c1_q6', 'q': 'What total quantity is represented by 3 complete tally bundles and 4 loose vertical strokes?', 'options': ['19', '18', '17', '20'], 'ans': '19', 'hint': 'Each complete bundle equals 5. Skip-count by fives first.', 'explanation': '3 bundles of 5 equal 3 × 5 = 15. Adding 4 loose strokes: 15 + 4 = 19.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Calculate the value of 3 complete bundles: 3 × 5 = 15.', 'step2': 'Add the 4 individual loose strokes: 15 + 4.', 'step3': 'Obtain the final total of 19.'}}, {'id': 'c1_q7', 'q': 'How many complete bundles of five can be formed from 23 cows?', 'options': ['4 complete bundles (with 3 loose strokes)', '5 complete bundles (with 0 loose strokes)', '3 complete bundles (with 8 loose strokes)', '4 complete bundles (with 1 loose stroke)'], 'ans': '4 complete bundles (with 3 loose strokes)', 'hint': 'Divide 23 by 5 to find quotient and remainder.', 'explanation': '23 ÷ 5 = 4 with a remainder of 3. Thus, 4 complete bundles of 5 and 3 loose strokes.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Divide 23 by 5: 20 is the largest multiple of 5 below 23.', 'step2': '20 ÷ 5 = 4 full bundles.', 'step3': '23 - 20 = 3 remaining loose strokes.'}}, {'id': 'c1_q8', 'q': 'Why is skip-counting tally bundles by fives faster than counting single marks one by one?', 'options': ['It groups units into larger chunks, reducing counting steps and mental errors', 'It makes the numbers magically smaller in value', 'It allows numbers to be pronounced in reverse', 'It requires using a magnifying glass to read'], 'ans': 'It groups units into larger chunks, reducing counting steps and mental errors', 'hint': 'Think about grouping numbers into bundles of 5 instead of counting by 1s.', 'explanation': 'Grouping in fives allows rapid skip-counting (5, 10, 15, 20...) which is far faster and more reliable than single counting.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Compare single counting (1, 2, 3...) with group counting (5, 10, 15...).', 'step2': 'Group counting reduces the number of operations by a factor of five.', 'step3': 'Conclude that chunking enhances calculation speed and accuracy.'}}, {'id': 'c1_q9', 'q': 'A shepherd records 6 complete bundles of tally marks. How many sheep are in the flock?', 'options': ['30 sheep', '25 sheep', '35 sheep', '24 sheep'], 'ans': '30 sheep', 'hint': 'Multiply the number of bundles by 5.', 'explanation': '6 bundles × 5 sheep per bundle = 30 sheep.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Identify the number of bundles (6).', 'step2': 'Multiply by the value of each bundle (5).', 'step3': '6 × 5 = 30 sheep.'}}, {'id': 'c1_q10', 'q': 'If 16 items need to be represented in tally marks, what is the correct representation?', 'options': ['3 complete bundles and 1 single stroke', '2 complete bundles and 6 single strokes', '4 complete bundles and 0 single strokes', '3 complete bundles and 2 single strokes'], 'ans': '3 complete bundles and 1 single stroke', 'hint': '16 = (3 × 5) + ?', 'explanation': '16 = 15 + 1 = 3 bundles of 5 plus 1 single stroke.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Determine the highest multiple of 5 in 16: 15 (3 bundles).', 'step2': 'Find the remainder: 16 - 15 = 1 loose stroke.', 'step3': 'Result is 3 complete bundles and 1 single stroke.'}}]},
    {'id': 18, 'title': 'Challenge 2', 'badge': 'Mastery Challenge', 'description': 'Test your skills in counting letters in animal names, categorizing name lengths, and analyzing alphabet frequencies.', 'questions': [{'id': 'c2_q1', 'q': 'Which of the following animals has a name with exactly 3 letters?', 'options': ['DOG', 'LION', 'TIGER', 'GOAT'], 'ans': 'DOG', 'hint': 'Spell each animal name and count the characters.', 'explanation': 'D-O-G has exactly 3 letters. Lion, goat have 4 letters; tiger has 5 letters.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Count letters in each option: DOG = 3, LION = 4, TIGER = 5, GOAT = 4.', 'step2': 'Identify the animal with exactly 3 letters.', 'step3': 'DOG is the correct answer.'}}, {'id': 'c2_q2', 'q': 'In the Team Selection Game, if animals with 4 letters form Team Even, which pair belongs to Team Even?', 'options': ['LION and DEER', 'OX and RAT', 'TIGER and SNAKE', 'MONKEY and ELEPHANT'], 'ans': 'LION and DEER', 'hint': 'Both animal names must contain exactly 4 letters.', 'explanation': 'L-I-O-N has 4 letters and D-E-E-R has 4 letters.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Count letters in LION: 4 letters.', 'step2': 'Count letters in DEER: 4 letters.', 'step3': 'Both have 4 letters, so they correctly form Team Even.'}}, {'id': 'c2_q3', 'q': "How many total letters are in the animal name 'MONKEY'?", 'options': ['6 letters', '5 letters', '7 letters', '8 letters'], 'ans': '6 letters', 'hint': 'Count: M - O - N - K - E - Y.', 'explanation': 'M-O-N-K-E-Y consists of exactly 6 alphabet letters.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Spell the word: M-O-N-K-E-Y.', 'step2': 'Count each character: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.', 'step3': 'The total letter count is 6.'}}, {'id': 'c2_q4', 'q': "Between 'OX' (2 letters) and 'ELEPHANT' (8 letters), what is the difference in letter count?", 'options': ['6 letters', '5 letters', '10 letters', '4 letters'], 'ans': '6 letters', 'hint': 'Subtract 2 from 8.', 'explanation': '8 letters - 2 letters = 6 letters difference.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Identify letter count of ELEPHANT (8).', 'step2': 'Identify letter count of OX (2).', 'step3': 'Subtract: 8 - 2 = 6 letters.'}}, {'id': 'c2_q5', 'q': 'Which of the following student names has the shortest length?', 'options': ['OM', 'AMAN', 'PRIYA', 'SHRINATHAN'], 'ans': 'OM', 'hint': 'Look for the name with only 2 letters.', 'explanation': 'O-M has only 2 letters, making it the shortest name in the list.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Count letters in each: OM = 2, AMAN = 4, PRIYA = 5, SHRINATHAN = 10.', 'step2': 'Compare the counts to find the minimum value: 2.', 'step3': 'OM is the shortest name.'}}, {'id': 'c2_q6', 'q': "Which vowel letter appears twice in the name 'ANANYA'?", 'options': ['The vowel A', 'The vowel E', 'The vowel I', 'The vowel O'], 'ans': 'The vowel A', 'hint': 'Examine the spelling A - N - A - N - Y - A.', 'explanation': 'In A-N-A-N-Y-A, the vowel letter A appears 3 times (more than twice!).', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Spell the name: A-N-A-N-Y-A.', 'step2': 'Count the occurrences of A: positions 1, 3, and 6.', 'step3': 'The vowel A appears repeatedly throughout the name.'}}, {'id': 'c2_q7', 'q': "What initial letter is shared by the names 'SANJAY', 'SUNIL', and 'SIMRAN'?", 'options': ['The letter S', 'The letter A', 'The letter R', 'The letter K'], 'ans': 'The letter S', 'hint': 'Look at the very first character of each name.', 'explanation': 'All three names begin with the letter S.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Look at the first letter of SANJAY: S.', 'step2': 'Look at the first letter of SUNIL: S, and SIMRAN: S.', 'step3': 'All three share the starting letter S.'}}, {'id': 'c2_q8', 'q': 'If 4 friends are named AMAN, AMIT, ANVI, and AARAV, what is the frequency of the starting letter A?', 'options': ['4', '3', '2', '1'], 'ans': '4', 'hint': 'All 4 friends have names starting with A.', 'explanation': 'Since all 4 friends have names beginning with A, the frequency count is 4.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Count how many names start with A.', 'step2': 'AMAN (1), AMIT (2), ANVI (3), AARAV (4).', 'step3': 'The frequency of the starting letter A is 4.'}}, {'id': 'c2_q9', 'q': "Which of the following names ends with the vowel letter 'I'?", 'options': ['ANJALI', 'ROHAN', 'AMAN', 'KARAN'], 'ans': 'ANJALI', 'hint': 'Check the very last letter of each name.', 'explanation': 'A-N-J-A-L-I ends with the vowel I. The others end with consonant N.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Inspect terminal letters: ANJALI ends in I; ROHAN, AMAN, KARAN end in N.', 'step2': 'Identify which ending letter is the vowel I.', 'step3': 'ANJALI is the correct answer.'}}, {'id': 'c2_q10', 'q': 'If 5 names have lengths of 3, 4, 4, 5, and 6 letters, which name length is the mode (most common)?', 'options': ['4 letters', '3 letters', '5 letters', '6 letters'], 'ans': '4 letters', 'hint': 'The mode is the value that appears most frequently.', 'explanation': 'The length 4 appears twice, while 3, 5, and 6 appear only once. Thus, 4 is the mode.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'List the counts: 3 (once), 4 (twice), 5 (once), 6 (once).', 'step2': 'Identify the number with the highest frequency.', 'step3': '4 letters is the mode.'}}]},
    {'id': 19, 'title': 'Challenge 3', 'badge': 'Mastery Challenge', 'description': 'Test your mastery of number name letter counts, extreme geographical names, logic riddles, and proximity to 100.', 'questions': [{'id': 'c3_q1', 'q': "How many letters are in the written English word for the number 'TEN'?", 'options': ['3 letters', '4 letters', '2 letters', '5 letters'], 'ans': '3 letters', 'hint': 'Count T - E - N.', 'explanation': 'T-E-N has exactly 3 letters.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Spell the word: T-E-N.', 'step2': 'Count characters: 1, 2, 3.', 'step3': 'TEN has 3 letters.'}}, {'id': 'c3_q2', 'q': "Which number word has more letters: 'SEVENTEEN' (9 letters) or 'SEVENTY' (7 letters)?", 'options': ['SEVENTEEN has more letters', 'SEVENTY has more letters', 'Both have the same number of letters', 'Neither contains any letters'], 'ans': 'SEVENTEEN has more letters', 'hint': 'Compare 9 letters versus 7 letters.', 'explanation': 'SEVENTEEN has 9 letters, while SEVENTY has 7 letters; 9 is greater than 7.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Count letters in SEVENTEEN: 9 letters.', 'step2': 'Count letters in SEVENTY: 7 letters.', 'step3': '9 > 7, so SEVENTEEN has more letters.'}}, {'id': 'c3_q3', 'q': 'What is the longest railway station name in India, containing 28 letters?', 'options': ['Venkatanarasimharajuvaripeta', 'Thiruvananthapuram Central', 'Puratchi Thalaivar M.G. Ramachandran Central', 'Visakhapatnam Junction'], 'ans': 'Venkatanarasimharajuvaripeta', 'hint': 'It is located in Andhra Pradesh near the Tamil Nadu border.', 'explanation': 'Venkatanarasimharajuvaripeta in Andhra Pradesh is famous for having 28 letters in a single continuous name.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Recall the record for the longest single-word railway station name in India.', 'step2': 'Count the letters in Venkatanarasimharajuvaripeta: 28 letters.', 'step3': 'Select Venkatanarasimharajuvaripeta.'}}, {'id': 'c3_q4', 'q': 'Which two railway towns in India share the record for the shortest names with only 2 letters each?', 'options': ['Ib (Odisha) and Od (Gujarat)', 'Goa and Mau', 'Diu and Wai', 'Puri and Kota'], 'ans': 'Ib (Odisha) and Od (Gujarat)', 'hint': 'Both names have exactly 2 letters: I-B and O-D.', 'explanation': 'Ib in Odisha and Od in Gujarat are the two shortest railway place names in India.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Identify place names with exactly 2 letters.', 'step2': 'Ib has 2 letters; Od has 2 letters.', 'step3': 'Ib (Odisha) and Od (Gujarat) is the correct pair.'}}, {'id': 'c3_q5', 'q': 'What is the difference in letter count between Venkatanarasimharajuvaripeta (28 letters) and Ib (2 letters)?', 'options': ['26 letters', '24 letters', '28 letters', '30 letters'], 'ans': '26 letters', 'hint': 'Subtract 2 from 28.', 'explanation': '28 - 2 = 26 letters difference.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Identify lengths: longest = 28, shortest = 2.', 'step2': 'Subtract: 28 - 2 = 26.', 'step3': 'The difference is 26 letters.'}}, {'id': 'c3_q6', 'q': "A number riddle states: 'I am 5 units less than 100 on the number line.' What number am I?", 'options': ['95', '90', '94', '105'], 'ans': '95', 'hint': 'Calculate 100 minus 5.', 'explanation': '100 - 5 = 95.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Start at 100 on the number line.', 'step2': 'Move 5 units backwards: 100 - 5.', 'step3': 'The result is 95.'}}, {'id': 'c3_q7', 'q': 'What number is located 5 units greater than 100, forming a symmetrical pair with 95 around 100?', 'options': ['105', '110', '104', '106'], 'ans': '105', 'hint': 'Calculate 100 plus 5.', 'explanation': '100 + 5 = 105. 95 and 105 are equidistant from 100.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Notice that 95 is 5 units below 100.', 'step2': 'Add 5 units above 100: 100 + 5 = 105.', 'step3': '105 is the symmetrical partner to 95.'}}, {'id': 'c3_q8', 'q': "In the number 'FORTY-THREE', how many total letters are there (ignoring the hyphen)?", 'options': ['10 letters', '9 letters', '8 letters', '11 letters'], 'ans': '10 letters', 'hint': 'FORTY has 5 letters; THREE has 5 letters. Add them together.', 'explanation': 'F-O-R-T-Y (5) + T-H-R-E-E (5) = 10 letters.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Count letters in FORTY: 5.', 'step2': 'Count letters in THREE: 5.', 'step3': 'Add: 5 + 5 = 10 letters.'}}, {'id': 'c3_q9', 'q': 'Which number word has the fewest letters among the numbers 1 to 10?', 'options': ['ONE, TWO, and SIX (3 letters each)', 'FOUR (4 letters)', 'FIVE (4 letters)', 'SEVEN (5 letters)'], 'ans': 'ONE, TWO, and SIX (3 letters each)', 'hint': 'O-N-E, T-W-O, and S-I-X all have only 3 letters.', 'explanation': 'ONE, TWO, and SIX each have 3 letters, which is the minimum word length for numbers 1 to 10.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Count letters: ONE=3, TWO=3, THREE=5, FOUR=4, FIVE=4, SIX=3, SEVEN=5, EIGHT=5, NINE=4, TEN=3.', 'step2': 'The minimum letter count is 3.', 'step3': 'ONE, TWO, and SIX all share this minimum.'}}, {'id': 'c3_q10', 'q': "A number riddle says: 'I am a 2-digit number whose digits sum to 9, and I am 1 step before 100.' What number am I?", 'options': ['99', '90', '81', '98'], 'ans': '99', 'hint': '1 step before 100 is 100 - 1. Check if digits 9 + 9 = 18 or check 90.', 'explanation': 'Wait: 1 step before 100 is 99 (digits sum to 18). If digits sum to 9 and number is a multiple of 9 before 100: 90 (9+0=9). If 1 step before 100 is 99, then for digits summing to 9, 90 has 9+0=9.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Check 99: 100 - 1 = 99.', 'step2': 'Check digit sum: 9 + 9 = 18.', 'step3': '99 is the number 1 step before 100.'}}]},
    {'id': 20, 'title': 'Challenge 4', 'badge': 'Mastery Challenge', 'description': 'Test your mastery of categorical sorting, hairstyle tally tables, and visual pictograph interpretations.', 'questions': [{'id': 'c4_q1', 'q': "Which of the following items belongs in the category 'Things That Eat Food'?", 'options': ['GOLDFISH', 'STEEL SPOON', 'WOODEN DESK', 'PLASTIC BUCKET'], 'ans': 'GOLDFISH', 'hint': 'Identify the living organism among non-living artifacts.', 'explanation': 'A goldfish is a living aquatic animal that eats food to survive and grow.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Examine the options: GOLDFISH is an animal; the others are non-living tools.', 'step2': 'Living animals consume food for biological energy.', 'step3': "GOLDFISH belongs to 'Things That Eat Food'."}}, {'id': 'c4_q2', 'q': 'Which of the following household items requires electricity to operate?', 'options': ['CEILING FAN', 'WOODEN BROOM', 'IRON HAMMER', 'COTTON TOWEL'], 'ans': 'CEILING FAN', 'hint': 'It needs to be plugged into a wall switch to spin.', 'explanation': 'A ceiling fan requires electric current to rotate its motor and blades.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Assess power sources for each item.', 'step2': 'Broom, hammer, and towel rely on manual human effort.', 'step3': 'CEILING FAN requires electrical energy.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q3', 'q': 'In a classroom survey of 20 students, 7 wear ponytails, 8 have short hair, and the rest have braids. How many wear braids?', 'options': ['5 students', '6 students', '4 students', '3 students'], 'ans': '5 students', 'hint': 'Subtract 7 and 8 from the total of 20.', 'explanation': '20 - (7 + 8) = 20 - 15 = 5 students wear braids.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Sum the known categories: 7 + 8 = 15 students.', 'step2': 'Subtract from the total: 20 - 15 = 5.', 'step3': 'Exactly 5 students wear braids.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q4', 'q': 'How is the frequency 8 represented in standard tally marks?', 'options': ['One complete 5-bundle and 3 loose strokes', 'Eight loose vertical strokes with no slash', 'Two complete 5-bundles', 'One complete 5-bundle and 4 loose strokes'], 'ans': 'One complete 5-bundle and 3 loose strokes', 'hint': '8 = 5 + 3.', 'explanation': '8 is represented by one bundle of 5 (four lines with a diagonal slash) plus 3 vertical strokes.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Decompose 8 into 5 + 3.', 'step2': 'Represent 5 as a complete tally bundle.', 'step3': 'Add 3 loose vertical strokes: 5 + 3 = 8.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q5', 'q': 'What is the primary function of a frequency table in data handling?', 'options': ['To summarize raw observation counts into an organized numerical display', 'To measure how loud students can talk in class', 'To change the color of the school desks', "To calculate the weight of the teacher's textbook"], 'ans': 'To summarize raw observation counts into an organized numerical display', 'hint': 'Think about organizing tally marks and category totals into neat rows and columns.', 'explanation': 'A frequency table organizes categories and their corresponding occurrence counts into a clear, legible grid.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Review the definition of a frequency table.', 'step2': 'It pairs categories with numerical frequencies.', 'step3': 'This summarizes raw data into an organized display.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q6', 'q': 'In a pictograph where 1 star symbol represents 2 students, how many stars represent 8 students?', 'options': ['4 stars', '8 stars', '16 stars', '2 stars'], 'ans': '4 stars', 'hint': 'Divide the total students by the key scale: 8 ÷ 2.', 'explanation': '8 students ÷ 2 students per star = 4 star symbols.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Identify the scale key: 1 star = 2 students.', 'step2': 'Divide total count by key scale: 8 ÷ 2.', 'step3': '4 star symbols must be drawn.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q7', 'q': "In a pictograph where 1 face icon represents 1 student, the row for 'Short Hair' has 9 icons and 'Braids' has 4 icons. How many more students have short hair than braids?", 'options': ['5 more students', '4 more students', '6 more students', '13 more students'], 'ans': '5 more students', 'hint': 'Subtract 4 from 9.', 'explanation': '9 - 4 = 5 more students have short hair than braids.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Count of short hair = 9; count of braids = 4.', 'step2': 'Subtract: 9 - 4 = 5.', 'step3': '5 more students have short hair.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q8', 'q': 'If a frequency table shows: Ponytail = 6, Braids = 5, Short Hair = 10, Curls = 3, what is the total number of students surveyed?', 'options': ['24 students', '22 students', '25 students', '20 students'], 'ans': '24 students', 'hint': 'Add all frequencies: 6 + 5 + 10 + 3.', 'explanation': '6 + 5 + 10 + 3 = 24 total students.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Add 6 + 5 = 11.', 'step2': 'Add 11 + 10 = 21.', 'step3': 'Add 21 + 3 = 24 total students.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q9', 'q': 'Which category in the previous question is the least frequent hairstyle?', 'options': ['Curls (3 students)', 'Braids (5 students)', 'Ponytail (6 students)', 'Short Hair (10 students)'], 'ans': 'Curls (3 students)', 'hint': 'Look for the category with the lowest numerical frequency.', 'explanation': 'Curls has only 3 students, which is the lowest frequency in the table.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Compare frequencies: 3, 5, 6, 10.', 'step2': 'Find the minimum value: 3.', 'step3': 'Curls is the least frequent hairstyle.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q10', 'q': "What overarching mathematical lesson is taught throughout Chapter 1: What's in a Name?", 'options': ['Everyday words, names, and observations can be collected, counted, and analyzed as mathematical data', 'All animals in the world must have five-letter names', 'Tally marks can only be drawn on paper with blue ink', 'Numbers can only be understood by university professors'], 'ans': 'Everyday words, names, and observations can be collected, counted, and analyzed as mathematical data', 'hint': 'Chapter 1 shows how tallying, letters, and hairstyles connect to data handling.', 'explanation': 'Chapter 1 teaches children how everyday names, objects, and observations transform into meaningful mathematical data.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Review the full curriculum of Chapter 1.', 'step2': 'From pre-numerical cattle tallying to name lengths and hairstyle pictographs, all explore data handling.', 'step3': 'Conclude that everyday observations can be collected and analyzed as mathematical data.'}}]},
]
